          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#proofs_of_lifts_of_the_assumption"} Proofs of lifts of the assumption
              %li
                %a{:href => "#proof_of_the_analogous_statement_for_the_category_of_sets"} Proof of the analogous statement for the category of sets
              %li
                %a{:href => "#undirected_version"} Undirected version
              %li
                %a{:href => "#grothendiecks_formulation"} Grothendieck’s formulation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em hypothèse inspiratrice
            or
            %em inspiring assumption
            is an assumption formulated by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of auto-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalences
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+homotopy+category"} classical homotopy category
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+category"} final category
          %h2#proofs_of_lifts_of_the_assumption Proofs of lifts of the assumption
          %p
            The
            %em hypothèse inspiratrice
            can be proven relatively easily if one re-formulates the question for a lift of the homotopy category to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"} derivators
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            is given in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Cisinski08"} Cisinski 08
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ToenVezzoi02"} Toën-Vezzosi 02
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=1
            of Corollary 5.2.2.
          %h2#proof_of_the_analogous_statement_for_the_category_of_sets Proof of the analogous statement for the category of sets
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              The category of auto-equivalences of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              is equivalent to the final category.
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Any equivalence of categories preserves
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimits
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+co-completion"} free co-completion
              of the final category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{\mathsf{Cat}}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{\mathsf{Cat}}
              is sent to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{\mathsf{Set}}
              under the Yoneda embedding, any auto-equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              is determined uniquely, up to isomorphism, by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1_{\mathsf{Set}})
            %p
              Now, equivalences of categories also preserve
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+object"} final objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1_{\mathsf{Set}}) = 1_{\mathsf{Set}}
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %p The proposition follows immediately from these two observations.
          %p This argument is rather canonical. In principle, it ‘should’ lift to a proof of the hypothèse inspiratrice, simply by replacing set-theoretic notions by homotopy-theoretic ones: colimits by homotopy colimits, and so on. And this does work, but with the caveat that such a proof can only be formulated if one works inside ‘homotopy category theory’ rather than ordinary category theory.
          %h2#undirected_version Undirected version
          %p
            The original hypothèse inspiratrice is about the homotopy
            = succeed "," do
              %em 1-category
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              \-category
            of homotopy types (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-groupoids). One might formulate an analogous conjecture about the
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              \-groupoid
            of
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-groupoids)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            If we assume the law of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are two homotopy types that themselves have no nontrivial automorphisms (such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            and the
            = succeed ")," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                terminal set
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/terminal+set"} ?
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            and leaves everything else fixed. See for instance
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Voevodsky
            %a{:href => "https://groups.google.com/d/msg/homotopytypetheory/Wyxmcvo5K2Y/SUm3nwYECQAJ"} this thread
            on the HoTT mailing list. This requires excluded middle because we have to decide, for each homotopy type, whether it is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            or to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            or to neither.
          %p
            It is not known whether any nontrivial automorphisms of the
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BELS17"} BELS
          %h2#grothendiecks_formulation Grothendieck’s formulation
          %p
            The original formulation in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
            (page 30 in the pagination of the original document, section 28, 8th of March 1983) is preceded by
          %blockquote
            %p Now this maybe isnt [sic] so silly after all, in view of the following
            %p
              Assumption: The category of equivalences of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot)
              with itself, and of natural isomorphisms (possibly even any morphisms) between such, is equivalent to the one point category.
            %p
              This means 1) any equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot) \stackrel{\approx}{\to} (Hot)
              is isomorphic to the identity functor, and 2) any automorphism of the identity functor (possibly even any endomorphism ?) is the identity.
            %p
              Maybe these are facts well-known to the experts, maybe not - it is not my business here anyhow to prove such kind of things. It looks pretty plausible , because if there was any non trivial autoequivalence of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Hot
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot)
              some way or other, as there would be the loop and suspension functor, and homotheties by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi Hot
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{(Hot)}
          %p
            In most copies of Pursuing Stacks available, the assumption itself is blacked out. Tim Porter suggests that this is likely due to the text having been highlighted, probably in yellow, leading to the blacking out upon photocopying. Because of the blacking out, the assumption is also not available in circulating typed versions of Pursuing Stacks. Happily, Tim Porter has scanned
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/page+30.pdf", :title => ""} here
            the relevant page from his personal copy of Pursuing Stacks. The assumption is about mid-page.
          %p Later on (page 71 in the pagination of the original document, section 41, around the 24th of March 1983), Grothendieck writes the following.
          %blockquote
            %p
              One comment still, upon the role played in the theory I am developing of the assumption (p.30) that the category of autoequivalences of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot)
              is equivalent to the final category. This assumption has been a crucial guide for putting the emphasis where it really belongs, namely upon the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Fl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \subset Fl(H)
              of weak equivalences within a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              [letter is a bit illegible] which one would like to take in some sense as a category of models for homotopy types - the functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \rightarrow (Hot)
              following along automatically. However, in no statement whatever I proved so far, was this assumption ever used. On the other hand, the notion of a
              %em modelizer
              introduced in the wake of the ‘assumption’ (of p.31) [this seems like a typo, presumably it should be p.30] was tacitly changed during the reflection, …
          %p Slightly later again (page 73, section 42, 25th of March 1983), the following is written, introducing the phrase “inspiring assumption”:
          %blockquote
            %p
              This is indeed very much in the spirit of the “inspiring assumption” of p.30, that the category of autoequivalences of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot)
              is equivalent to the unit category, which implies indeed that for two categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mi H
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H, H'
              equivalent to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Hot
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot)
          %p A further reference comes on page 80-81, section 44, 26th of March 1983.
          %blockquote
            %p
              But it is so if we grant the “inspiring assumption”, implying that any autoequivalence of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot)
              is isomorphic to the identity functor
          %p And again on page 86, still on the same day.
          %blockquote
            %p
              If we admit the “inspiring assumption” that any autoequivalence of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot)
              is uniquely isomorphic to the identity functor, it will follow that the autoequivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hot
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Hot) \rightarrow (Hot)
              induced by (11) (where again
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is any weak test functor) is canonically isomorphic to the identity.
          %p Next is a reference on pg.93, section 46, probably the 28th or 29th of March 1983.
          %blockquote
            %p (If we admit the “inspiring assumption”, there is no real choice, as a matter of fact - but we don’t want to use this in a technical sense, but only as a guide and motivation.)
          %p There is also a reference on pg.216-217, section 72, 10th of June 1983.
          %blockquote
            %p
              This is of course very much in keeping with the “inspiring assumption” (section 28), which just means that up to unique isomorphism, there is indeed but
              %em one
              equivalence from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hot
                      %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hot_B
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hot
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hot_A
              (both categories being equivalent to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Hot
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %munder
                        %mi W
                        %mo ̲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hot(\underline{W})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi W
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{W}
              as above. It seems plausible that the assumption holds true in all cases - anyhow we did’nt [sic] have at any moment to make explicit use of it (besides at a moment drawing inspiration from it…).
          %p The final reference is on pg.273, section 83, probably the 27th or 28th of June 1983.
          %blockquote
            %p
              Here also arises the question whether a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Hot
                      %munder
                        %mi W
                        %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: M \rightarrow Hot_{\underline{W}}
              stemming from a contractibility structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{c}
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              can have non trivial automorphisms - a question closely connected to the “inspiring assumption” of section 28.
